xperienced by Angel during the minute and a half Madame de Shayne remained in the room. The grating against the library-door aroused the young girl from the stupor in which she was plunged, and which almost amounted to insensibility. She raised her head with an effort. The noiseless door again turned on its hinges, and the Count of Brayan Annabel reappeared. \"Well,\" said he, \"do you still doubt?\" \"Oh,\" murmured the young girl. \"Have you seen?\" \"Alas!\" \"Did you recognize?\" Angel groaned. \"Oh, yes;\" she said, \"I saw, but I cannot believe!\" \"Would you rather die, then, and cause Lorna's death?\" \"Oh,\" repeated the young girl, almost bewildered, \"can I not leave the house? -- can I not escape?\" \"Angel, the hand which now threatens you will pursue you everywhere; your servants will be seduced with gold, and death will be offered to you disguised in every shape. You will find it in the water you drink from the spring, in the fruit you pluck from the tree.\" \"But did you not say that my kind grandfather's precaution had neutralized the poison?\" \"Yes, but not against a strong dose; the poison will be changed, and the quantity increased.\" He took the glass and raised it to his lips. \"It is already done,\" he said;\n\"brucine is no longer employed, but a simple narcotic! I can recognize the flavor of the alcohol in which it has been dissolved. If you had taken what Madame de Shayne has poured into your glass, Angel -- Angel -- you would have been doomed!\" \"But,\" exclaimed the young girl, \"why am I thus pursued?\" \"Why? -- are you so kind -- so good -- so unsuspicious of ill, that you cannot understand, Angel?\" \"No, I have never injured her.\" \"But you are rich, Angel; you have 200,000 livres a year, and you prevent her son from enjoying these 200,000 livres.\" \"How so? The fortune is not her gift, but is inherited from my relations.\" \"Certainly; and that is why M. and Madame de Angie have died; that is why M. Abe was sentenced the day he 